{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Remember.</b> The reason for \"sanctify it\" is the verse \"six days you shall labor\" (v. 9), and thus he explained \"and He sanctified it,\" that HaShem rested on it from all His work. And the reason for its being joined with \"bless HaShem\" is as written in the section \"Thus,\" for HaShem sanctified this day and designated it for souls to receive an addition of wisdom more than all the other days; therefore it is written \"bless HaShem.\" I have already explained this in the psalm for the Sabbath day. We see that the Sabbatical year resembles the Sabbath, for it too is seventh among the years, and HaShem commanded that the Torah be read at the beginning of the year before men, women, and children, and stated the reason: \"that they may hear and that they may learn, etc., and observe\" (Deuteronomy 31:12). Now the Sabbath was given to understand the deeds of HaShem and to meditate on His Torah, and thus it is written: \"for You, HaShem, have made me glad through Your work\" (Psalms 92:5). All the days of the week a person is occupied with his needs, but this day is fitting for seclusion and rest for the honor of HaShem; he shall not engage in vain even in his past needs or what he plans to do, and thus the prophet said: \"from pursuing your own pleasure and speaking a word\" (Isaiah 58:13). The custom of Israel was to go near the Sabbath to the prophets, as in \"why will you go to him today? It is neither new moon nor Sabbath\" (II Kings 4:23). I have already mentioned that one who does any work on the Sabbath denies the act of Creation but does not deny the honored HaShem. Now there is no doubt that the word \"you\" includes everyone who is subject to commandments; therefore the reason for \"your son and your daughter\" — the minor ones — is that their rest depends on you, and you are obligated to guard them lest they do anything that is forbidden for you to do. And similarly \"your male servant and your maidservant\" — whoever is under your authority, you are obligated to guard him and not allow him to work for another, and if not, you transgress a negative commandment — \"that your male servant and your maidservant may rest as you do\" (Deuteronomy 5), as Moses our lord explained in the manner I mentioned. On this condition the ger may reside in your gates — that he not do work on the Sabbath and on the Day of Atonements; therefore in the second [set of commandments] the ger is written, and likewise with forbidden sexual relations — on this condition he may reside, that he observe the forbidden relations, and similarly with eating blood. And we see in Jeremiah that he was stringent regarding the commandment of the Sabbath, and stated explicitly: even though a decree has been issued that Jerusalem be destroyed, if Israel repents to keep the Sabbath as they were commanded, they will not be exiled from their place, and thrones of the kingdom of the house of David will stand in Jerusalem."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Resolved מ\"א ד כג (=II Kings 4:23) based on the quoted verse text; also resolved the abbreviated chapter references to מלכים א and standard citation format."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:36:20.634600783Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}